From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiang Tian <ot_jiang.tian@mediatek.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Jan 2024 11:52:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Fix crash from asynchronous GPU metrics

Making the scope more accurate that only acquire the lock when trying
to access frame info in FrameInfoVisualizer, then make it irrelevant
to the real draw operation.

Bug: 317995179
Test: 1.going to developer options
      2. swapping the "profile hwui" option from "none" to "bars"
          and back a couple times, no crash

[nomorecoolnicknames: Android 13 port of AOSP e0edc3af8c35b616e21ada6989a1ccbcda1a1571
 https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/e0edc3af8c35b616e21ada6989a1ccbcda1a1571
 Not a clean cherry-pick: on 13 IRenderPipeline::draw has no
 HardwareBufferRenderParams argument, so every hunk was re-applied by
 hand with the same effect - CanvasContext::draw no longer holds the
 mutex across mRenderPipeline->draw(), the GL and Vulkan pipelines take
 it only around profiler->draw(), and the mutex is renamed
 mFrameInfoMutex as upstream. Port-only addition: <mutex> in
 IRenderPipeline.h, which now names std::mutex.
 Why on legacy MediaTek devices: the Mali-T720 r7p0 (Nougat) EGL dequeues
 the buffer inside the Skia flush, i.e. under this lock; SurfaceFlinger's
 completion callback (onSurfaceStatsAvailable) then blocks on it, the
 buffer release queued behind it on the same oneway binder node never
 arrives, and RenderThread waits in dequeueBuffer for the full 4000 ms
 HWUI timeout.]

Change-Id: I069a28a7e847c0c3fca94fd9c43e95382f501b80

diff --git a/libs/hwui/pipeline/skia/SkiaOpenGLPipeline.cpp b/libs/hwui/pipeline/skia/SkiaOpenGLPipeline.cpp
